  "script": {
    "segments": [
      {
        "narration": "Giant seven-foot geometric patterns kept appearing on Japan's seafloor. In 1995, divers off the coast of Amami Oshima discovered intricate circular ridges sculpted into the sand.",
        "visual_query": "amami oshima coral reef underwater",
        "visual_fallback": "tropical seabed sand"
      },
      {
        "narration": "The radial troughs were symmetrical, dotted with crushed shells, and vanished within days. Marine biologists had never documented anything like them.",
        "visual_query": "underwater geometric sand formation seafloor",
        "visual_fallback": "rippled ocean floor"
      },
      {
        "narration": "How could something create complex mathematical art in shifting ocean currents? Some suspected submarine testing, while others blamed unidentified underwater phenomena.",
        "visual_query": "scuba divers exploring sandy ocean floor",
        "visual_fallback": "deep ocean diving"
      },
      {
        "narration": "In 2011, researchers finally set up 24-hour cameras. The architect wasn't a team of engineers, but a five-inch male white-spotted pufferfish.",
        "visual_query": "white spotted pufferfish ocean floor",
        "visual_fallback": "pufferfish swimming seabed"
      },
      {
        "narration": "He spent ten days flapping his fins tirelessly, crafting sand channels to shield incoming eggs from strong ocean currents.",
        "visual_query": "pufferfish building sand nest underwater",
        "visual_fallback": "small fish on sand"
      }
    ],
